{
  "title": "Spree Commerce \u2014 eCommerce (15,427\u2605)",
  "content": "Project: Spree Commerce\nDomain: eCommerce | Scale: large | Stars: 15,427\nProblem: Spree is an open-source headless eCommerce platform that enables businesses to launch cross-border storefronts, B2B wholesale, multi-vendor marketplaces, and multi-tenant SaaS solutions without building from scratch.\nTarget users: eCommerce merchants, B2B wholesale businesses, Multi-vendor marketplace operators, SaaS eCommerce providers, Cross-border retailers\nCore features: REST API with TypeScript SDK and OpenAPI 3.0 spec; Production-ready Next.js storefront with multi-region routing and Stripe payments; Cross-border commerce with Markets, multi-currency, and EU Omnibus compliance; Provider-agnostic payment processing with Stripe, Adyen, and PayPal support; Advanced pricing and promotions with Price Lists, Customer Groups, and Gift Cards; Admin Dashboard with role-based permissions and Tailwind CSS customization; MeiliSearch integration for typo-tolerant product search and faceted filtering; Event Bus, Webhooks 2.0, and native integrations (GA4, GTM, Klaviyo)\nIntegrations: Stripe, Adyen, PayPal, MeiliSearch, Google Analytics 4, Google Tag Manager, Klaviyo, OpenAPI 3.0\nTech stack: Next.js 16, React 19, TypeScript, Tailwind CSS 4 + Ruby on Rails + PostgreSQL (inferred from Rails/Docker setup) + Docker, Render deployment, Turbo monorepo\nKey libraries: Spree gem, TypeScript SDK, @base-ui/react, MeiliSearch, Stripe SDK, Webhooks 2.0\nArchitecture: monolith\nDesign lessons:\n- Monorepo strategy using Turbo with clear separation: Ruby backend (spree-starter) and TypeScript frontend ecosystem enable independent scaling and team autonomy\n- Headless-first REST API design with flat JSON and publishable keys decouples storefront from backend, enabling multiple client implementations (Next.js, custom, etc.)\n- Provider-agnostic payment abstraction via Payment Sessions pattern allows swapping processors without storefront code changes, reducing vendor lock-in\n- Multi-tenant and multi-market support built into core (Markets bundle, Price Lists, Customer Groups) rather than added later, indicating platform designed for enterprise complexity",
